    read: 'The bounce ceiling and the descending line went in one bar on the largest volume of the window, and the line that flips the read was missed by 0.03. Nov 11 closed 1.35 (+1.5%). Nov 12 closed 1.63 (+20.7%) on $4.21M traded — 5.6× the 20-day average ($754K) and the 30-day window max (the previous max was $2.03M) — through 1.46 (the EMA20, the watch’s bounce ceiling) by 11% and the descending line from 4.39 (Apr 6) → 2.27 (Sep 17) (~1.55 today) by 5% in one session: the first close over that line since April, and the highest close of the last fifteen sessions (the 30-day window top is 1.81). The close is 0.018 (1.1%) under the EMA50 (1.648) and 1.8% under 1.66 — the invalidation: outside the near-miss rule by a tenth of a point against the average and by 0.8 against the line; not fired, the test. The volume half of the read’s trigger (a close over the line with 2× volume) printed at 5.6×; the price half did not. Score 3/7, BEAR, from 1/7: price over the EMA20, MACD histogram +0.03 and RSI 63.0 (from 38.2 — the first reading over 50 since the breakdown) the three checks on; under the EMA50 (1.648), EMA100 (1.93) and EMA200 (2.35), all falling. The engine keeps the "downtrend, lower highs, no floor" frame — its line is the one just closed through — and the BEAR forecast (invalidation 2.04, from 1.72; path 1.47 / 1.32 / 1.12 — its +1M is the EMA20, now under price). Volume: a 5.6× bar on a company of this size is news-sized and this data does not say what it was — the read is written from the bar. The 20-day average rose to $754K from $655K with it. The read stays BEAR by the rule — the line is 1.8% up, not printed — with the invalidation drifting to 1.65 with the average: a 5.6× bar that stops 0.03 under the first falling average is either the first bar of the reversal the read has asked for since October (the next close decides) or the largest one-day bounce of a downtrend; the read does not sell it into the average and does not buy it under the line.',
